Benjamin Lewis wrote:

> So the problem lies in the initial reading comprehension? :)

I think that's the case.

I remember finding out only a few months ago that the Dutch word
"firmanent" was actually spelled "firmament". I always thought the first
was correct, until someone pointed out to me that I was mistaken.

I'd been misreading that word for, say, some thirty years. I never
noticed I was wrong before, and even not, I have trouble accepting the
"new" spelling.

Some languages where spelling rules are formalised change their spelling
once in a while. I believe this poses less problems, since you will see
the new spelling all around you and you know that you should be aware of
the changes. Still, your "word image" is kind of fixed, I think, so a
change of spelling will always be a problem to most people.

Oddly, I think that dyslectics wouldn't have this problem, since they
have no fixed word image (actually, you might say that they would always
have this problem).

